\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The based plaquette under total inversion}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a finite periodic coordinate grid let #{U} be a
\vocabk{connection}{fdip-006D} in a unitary representation
on a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian fiber. Count one
positive elementary face at each site for each ordered choice
#{i<j} of two coordinate directions, keeping the cell labels
at all periods. Write #{H_{ij}(x;U)} for transport around
the steps #{+i,+j,-i,-j}, with the first step acting first.
The real-trace sum
##{s_{\square}(U)=\sum_{x}\sum_{i<j}\operatorname{Re}\trace H_{ij}(x;U)}
is invariant under vertex gauges and under the total link
inversion #{U^b_j(x)=U_j(-x-e_j)^{-1}}. Every real function
of #{s_{\square}(U)} gives an invariant scalar factor for
\ref{fdip-019F}.}

\proof{
\p{Put #{y=-x-e_i-e_j}, and abbreviate the positive edges
of the face at #{y} by
##{u=U_i(y),\quad v=U_j(y+e_i),\quad
 w=U_i(y+e_j),\quad z=U_j(y).}
The ordered holonomy of \ref{fdip-006F} applies the first
transport on the right, giving #{H=z^{-1}w^{-1}vu}.
At #{x} the corresponding #{4} positive edges for #{U^b}
in the same order of labels are #{w^{-1},z^{-1},u^{-1},v^{-1}}.
Consequently its holonomy is
##{vu z^{-1}w^{-1}=(vu)H(vu)^{-1}.}
This is a change of starting corner on the same oriented
face. The trace is invariant under conjugation.
For each #{i<j} the map #{x\mapsto y} is a bijection, giving
invariance after summation even for coincident vertices of
different labeled edges.}

\p{A vertex gauge acts on a source-to-head transport by left
multiplication at the head and inverse right multiplication at
the source. The interior gauge factors cancel around the
face, leaving conjugation at the base. Reversing a chosen
orientation would replace the unitary holonomy by its inverse
and conjugate its trace, so the real trace is also independent
of that choice. This argument gives the claimed scalar
factors on any inversion-stable class, in the represented
group of the connection. A spatial cubic example of this gauge
weight is \ref{fdip-00ND}.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.trace_conjTranspose} identifies
the trace of the adjoint matrix with the conjugate trace over
the complex numbers. For unitary holonomy its adjoint is its
inverse.}
